  <h2>Three documents that look alike</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>What it does</th><th>Who controls the claim</th><th>How careful to be</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Work authorization</strong></td><td>Permission to start and an agreement to pay for the work</td><td>You</td><td>Normal; read the payment terms</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Direction to pay</strong></td><td>Tells your insurer to pay the company directly</td><td>You</td><td>Usually fine; confirm it doesn't also assign rights</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Assignment of benefits (AOB)</strong></td><td>Transfers some of your policy rights for this loss to the company</td><td>Partly the company</td><td>Slow down; you can usually say no and still get the work</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The title on the page isn't always reliable. A document called "Work Authorization" can contain assignment language in the small print. Look for words like <em>assign</em>, <em>assignment</em>, <em>transfer</em> or <em>all rights and benefits</em>.</p>

  <h2>Five things to read before you sign anything</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>How the price is set.</strong> Is there an estimate, an hourly rate, or "pricing per the current industry estimating software"? Ask for an itemized estimate as soon as the scope is clear.</li>
    <li><strong>What you owe if insurance doesn't pay.</strong> Most contracts make you responsible for anything the insurer doesn't cover, plus your deductible. That's common; just know it.</li>
    <li><strong>Cancellation.</strong> How do you stop the work, and what do you pay for work already done and equipment already placed?</li>
    <li><strong>Liens.</strong> Contractors can often file a lien on your property if they aren't paid. Check what the contract says about disputes before it gets there.</li>
    <li><strong>Assignment language.</strong> If you see it, you can ask for a version without it, strike it before signing, or ask whether a direction to pay will do instead.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Your right to cancel</h2>
  <p>The FTC's <strong>Cooling-Off Rule</strong> generally gives you <strong>three business days to cancel</strong> a sale of $25 or more made at your home, and requires the seller to tell you about that right. But it has an <strong>emergency exception</strong>: if you need goods or services to meet a genuine emergency, you request them, and you sign a <strong>separate, dated statement in your own words</strong> describing the emergency and waiving the right to cancel, the three-day right doesn't apply.</p>
  <p>That's exactly the situation after a burst pipe. If a company asks you to sign an emergency waiver, it may be legitimate, but read it and keep a copy. Some states add their own home-solicitation or contractor rules; your state section on this site lists them.</p>

  <h2>About assignments of benefits</h2>
  <p>An AOB can be convenient: the company deals with the insurer, and you're not chasing payments. The trade-off is control. Depending on your state and the document, the contractor may negotiate, dispute, or even sue over that part of your claim, and you may have less say.</p>
  <p>Rules vary a lot by state. <strong>Florida</strong> prohibited assignment of post-loss benefits under residential and commercial property policies issued on or after <strong>January 1, 2023</strong>, after years of disputes and litigation. Other states allow AOBs, sometimes with required disclosures or cancellation rights. If you're unsure, call your state insurance department's consumer line before signing.</p>

  <h2>A calm way to handle the tablet in the driveway</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Say: "I'm happy to authorize emergency drying. Can I read this first?" Reputable companies expect that.</li>
    <li>Ask: "Is there anything in here that assigns my insurance benefits to you?"</li>
    <li>If yes, ask whether a <strong>direction to pay</strong> will do instead.</li>
    <li>Photograph or download a copy of everything you sign.</li>
    <li>If you feel pressured, it's fine to let them start <strong>emergency extraction only</strong> under a simple authorization and decide on the rest once you've talked to your insurer.</li>
  </ol>

